{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '545,650p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '885,955p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1579,1610p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; cat collab/1790522276330-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice8-confirm-r4.gpt-6-sol.findings.md; sed -n '210,235p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '310,365p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '475,710p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '515,535p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '110,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '545,650p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '885,955p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1579,1610p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; cat collab/1790522276330-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice8-confirm-r4.gpt-6-sol.findings.md; sed -n '210,235p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '310,365p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '475,710p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc; sed -n '515,535p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '110,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\n\nA parked machine was waiting for something. That something is a resource of\nthe emitter's host, and the resumer cannot poll it. `:yin.k/pending` records\nthe wait in resource-independent terms so the resumer can re-establish an\n*equivalent* wait, or decide it cannot. The first draft carried sketches;\nthe set here is exhaustive over §7.4.1's reasons, and each variant carries\neverything a *foreign* resumer needs — including the state the first draft\nleft implicit on the emitter's heap:\n\n```clojure\n;; blocked read (:stream-next)\n{:yin.k/reason   :next\n :yin.k/cell     :yin.k/c-17}          ; the cursor CELL id, §7.5.3 — not a bare position\n\n;; blocked write (:stream-put, or an effectful call's :stream/put)\n{:yin.k/reason   :put\n :yin.k/stream   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; the target\n :yin.k/value    <encoded>}            ; the retained value — a retry appends THIS, and\n                                       ; without it the wait is not re-establishable\n\n;; FFI call, sent (:ffi-call → ok)\n{:yin.k/reason     :ffi\n :yin.k/call-id    :call-7             ; the :dao.stream.apply/id correlation\n :yin.k/op         :op/add\n :yin.k/request    {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the request went\n :yin.k/response   {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}   ; where the answer lands\n :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23         ; the response cursor cell, §7.5.3, at its KEPT position}\n\n;; FFI call, retained (:ffi-call → full): the request is still in hand\n{:yin.k/reason     :ffi-request\n :yin.k/call-id    :call-7\n :yin.k/request-op :op/add\n :yin.k/request-args [<encoded> …]     ; enough to rebuild the envelope verbatim\n :yin.k/request    {identity/descriptor as above}\n :yin.k/response   {identity/descriptor as above}\n :yin.k/cell       :yin.k/c-23}        ; response cell at its kept position\n```\n\nThree rules make these resumable somewhere other than where they were minted:\n\n- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\n  cell through `dao.stream.remote.md`: the exporter enters the cell's\n  handle in its table and the cell's stream descriptor becomes a remote\n  descriptor. This is an access path to the original logical stream, not\n  a copied stream with new positions. `dao.stream` itself assumes no\n  network. The mirror serves the source's declared reader and writer\n  surface, preserving its outcome maps and cursor namespace rather than\n  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\n  cursor. `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` names\n  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, the one profile: a cursor is plain data in the\n  channel's portable domain and the source honors it after\n  serialization, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n  If the source cannot be entered in a table, preserve the cursor, or\n  keep a channel reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n  minting the value. A reflection marked gone on the receiver is also\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity. The exporter keeps\n  the entry served under a lease the resumer holds\n  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6). Re-homing a stream is out of\n  scope: serving from the exporter pins the exporter.\n- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n  value), where through a reflection `ok` is acceptance on the outbound\n  path and the wait resumes only when the source's `ok` is observed on the\n  link's event writer, while an append whose effect is unknown leaves the\n  wait undischarged for the program or its composition to decide\n  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 2.4 and 2.5); a `:next` wait polls\n  its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n  retained value is recomputed — what parks is what resumes.\n\n**Linker pending variants (M4).** Specified by `yin.vm.linker.md` and\nimplemented in M4. The require flow adds three pending variants, one per\nsafepoint of the same name (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2, 7.3). A wait entry's\n`:cursor` (the kept cursor) is UCF's `:yin.k/cell`: the cell id stands for\nthe cursor and its kept position rides in the cells table of section\n7.5.3:\n\n```clojure\n;; module link, request in hand (:module/require miss)\n{:yin.k/reason    :link-request\n :yin.k/link-id   [:t0 7]                 ; [origin counter], below\n :yin.k/envelope  {:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :yin.link/name 'foo\n                   :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code\n### 7.5.3 Sharing, cells, and cycles\n\n**The value table.** A configuration is a DAG, not a tree: a closure's\ncaptured environment is the frame's environment; a value on the stack is\nalso bound in `E`. Encoding naively duplicates, and a deep K makes\nduplication quadratic. The encoder therefore builds a **value table**:\n\n- On encountering a collection, closure, or continuation it has already\n  encoded (host identity during one lift), it emits\n  `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/ref :yin.k/id addr}` where `addr` is the `dao.jing`\n  address of the encoded subvalue, and places the subvalue in\n  `:yin.k/values {addr → encoded}`.\n- A subvalue is placed in the table when it is referenced more than once or\n  its encoded size exceeds a composition-supplied threshold; otherwise it\n  stays inline. Both choices yield equal semantics; only the table is needed\n  for correctness of sharing.\n- A cycle (a value reachable from itself through host identity) is refused\n  as `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :cyclic` naming the path. The\n  reference machine cannot produce one — `E` is extended by `merge`, never\n  mutated — so the refusal guards against foreign engines and bridge-returned\n  host structures. Cycles *through ref data* cannot be minted at all and are\n  rejected at decode (§7.5.1); this single representation retires the first\n  draft's two competing ones.\n\nBecause table entries are content-addressed, a carrier that already holds an\nentry (a `dao.jing` intake pool, a DHT replica) need not carry it again, and\ntwo continuations parked from the same closure share its encoding. This is\n`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3's checkpoint-as-published-fold applied to\na single frame. It is also why §7.3.4 separates `:yin.k/id` (which varies\nwith these choices, harmlessly) from `:yin.k/occurrence` (which does not).\n\n**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\ncursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\nadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\nadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\npolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\nwaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\nexactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\ntheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n;; values reference cells, never positions:\n{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n```\n\nCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\nlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\na tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\nerasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\nfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\none entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n\n**Cells lower into the private resource table (linker M4).** Specified by\n`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The lowering rule above -- each\ncell becomes a fresh store cursor entry -- is amended by the resource\nsplit of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3: each cell becomes a cursor entry in the\nreceiver's private `:resources` table, seeded with its carried position,\nand every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one\ncell still share one entry and one ref per cell keeps its own, but no\nstore key is ever created, and program values keep only the opaque\nreference ids, exactly as a running program holds them.\n\nA stream reference is portable when the exporter can enter its handle in a\n`dao.stream.remote.md` table and publish a remote descriptor for the\noriginal logical stream. This applies to local implementations,\n## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n\nThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\nclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\nthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n\n- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  windows of §7.7.5.\n- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on\n  `dao.stream.remote.md` and its **portable cursor profile**\n  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, which preserves the source stream's kept cursor\n  and `gap` outcomes. The M4 string-backed stream test below is the\n  required cross-host proof.\n- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\n  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\n  boundary declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; it cannot claim\n  exactly-once custody from the lease alone.\n- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`. Canonical CBOR landed in\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-27 20:07:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 03:07:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**P1 — the response route still occupies the receiver’s fixed cursor key.** The fix preserves the receiver’s call-in and call-out stream keys, and the retained retry uses a fresh reflection key ([remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:637)). But lowering installs the emitter’s response cell at `vm/call-out-cursor-key` ([remote.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:524)), which the engine also uses for every new call’s response wait ([semantic.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:106)). The engine resolves that wait through the cursor cell’s stream, so a new call’s response wait initially reads the emitter’s call-out ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:522)).\n\nThe two-call test reaches completion only after it manually replaces that cursor cell with the receiver’s own at lines 565–567 ([remote_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:559)). No slice 8 code performs that handoff. Replacing the cell can also redirect a co-waiter still sharing the migrated response cell, contrary to the cell-sharing rule ([design](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:916)). The test therefore does not establish correct routing for both waits without composition intervention.\n\nI did not edit files or rerun suites. I accept the supplied 18-test, 162-assertion results on JVM, Node, and Dart as evidence for the tested path.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nSign-off: DENIED\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Cell minting and remapping (S7.5.3), across the whole frame set\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- mint-cell\n  \"One mint per distinct cursor cell across the WHOLE frame set:\n   `found` accumulates the remapping {cell key -> UCF cell id} beside\n   the cells themselves as entries lift, so two waiters on one\n   engine cursor cell lift to one UCF cell, and independent cells\n   stay independent, whatever their positions (S7.5.3). `k` is the\n   cell's identity -- the engine cursor resource id a `:next` waiter\n   names, or the response stream and kept cursor a link wait names.\n   The cell carries the marker `serve!` mints for the cell's stream\n   and its kept cursor, published only after the cursor survives the\n   codec round trip; nil -- refuse -- when it does not, or when\n   `serve!` refuses the stream.\"\n  [serve! found]\n  (fn [codec k handle kept]\n    (or (get-in @found [:of k])\n        (let [portable (portable-cursor codec kept)\n              marker (when portable (lift-marker serve! handle))]\n          (when (and portable marker)\n            (let [cid (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"c-\" (count (:cells @found))))\n                  cell {:yin.k/stream marker, :yin.k/position portable}]\n\n\n(defn- call-out-cell\n  \"The emitter's call-out cursor cell -- the response route the\n   retained call had on the emitter, carried as a cell like any\n   other: the cursor entry under `vm/call-out-cursor-key`, reading\n   the call-out stream under `vm/call-out-stream-key` at its kept\n   position. It lowers under its own fresh key (S7.5.3), so a\n   response wait on the same call-out cursor shares it and distinct\n   routes stay distinct. nil when the composition holds no call\n   pair, which no real retained request does -- the pair is checked\n   before the call parked.\"\n  [resources]\n  (let [cursor (get resources vm/call-out-cursor-key)\n        handle (get resources vm/call-out-stream-key)]\n    (when (and (map? cursor) (contains? cursor :stream-id) handle)\n      {:id vm/call-out-cursor-key,\n       :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key,\n       :cursor (:cursor cursor)})))\n\n\n(defn- lift-retained-request\n  \"S7.4.3's retained call: a `:put` entry carrying `:request-sent`,\n   the request the engine holds unsent. The request stream's marker\n   is served, and `:request-op` and `:request-args` are read out of\n   the retained envelope so a resumer rebuilds it verbatim under the\n   call id. The emitter's call-out cursor rides beside them as a cell\n   (`call-out-cell`) at its kept position -- the response route the\n   call had on the emitter -- and lowers under its own fresh key like\n   any cell, never at a fixed key. `:yin.k/unsatisfied` when the\n   request stream cannot be served, or the route is missing or its\n   cursor does not survive the codec round trip.\"\n  [serve! resources mint codec entry]\n  (let [h (get resources (:stream-id entry))\n        envelope (:datom entry)\n        route (call-out-cell resources)]\n    (if-let [cid (when route\n                   (mint codec (:id route)\n                         (get resources (:stream-id route))\n                         (:cursor route)))]\n      (if-let [marker (lift-marker serve! h)]\n        {:pending {:yin.k/reason :ffi-request,\n                   :yin.k/call-id (:call-id entry),\n                   :yin.k/request-op (or (apply2/request-op envelope)\n                                         (:op entry)),\n                   :yin.k/request-args (apply2/request-args envelope),\n                   :yin.k/request marker,\n                   :yin.k/response-cell cid},\n         :cell? true}\n        (unsatisfied (local-identity h)))\n      (unsatisfied (local-identity\n                     (or (and route (get resources (:stream-id route)))\n                         h))))))\n\n\n(defn- lift-link\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- stream-id-of\n  \"The fresh resource id the reflection of the stream `identity`\n   names stands under: derived from the identity, so two markers of\n   one stream attach once and lower shared. A receiver composing\n   these entries into a live `:resources` table re-keys them -- the\n   ids are fresh for the lowered state, the remap mechanical.\"\n  [identity]\n  (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"s-\" identity)))\n\n\n(defn- pending-markers\n  \"Every stream marker `pending` names beside its cells' -- the\n   target of a put, the request of a retained call, the pair of a\n   link request.\"\n  [pending]\n  (case (:yin.k/reason pending)\n    :put [(:yin.k/stream pending)]\n    :ffi-request [(:yin.k/request pending)]\n    (:link-request :link-response)\n    [(:yin.k/request pending)\n     (:yin.k/response pending)]\n    []))\n\n\n(defn- attach-all\n  \"One attach per distinct stream identity, each ok installing its\n   reflection under `stream-id-of`'s fresh resource id. The first\n   attach that does not answer ok -- a reflection marked gone\n   included -- refuses the lower as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming that\n   identity.\"\n  [attach! markers]\n  (let [by-id (reduce (fn [m mkr]\n                        (update m (:dao.stream/identity mkr) #(or % mkr)))\n                      {} markers)]\n    (reduce-kv (fn [acc identity mkr]\n                 (if-let [h (lower-marker attach! mkr)]\n                   (let [sid (stream-id-of identity)]\n                     (-> acc\n                         (assoc-in [:streams identity] sid)\n                         (assoc-in [:resources sid] h)))\n                   (reduced (unsatisfied identity))))\n               {:streams {}, :resources {}}\n               by-id)))\n\n\n(defn- cell-resources\n  \"The fresh cursor entry each UCF cell lowers to: its carried\n   position, on the reflection of the stream the cell names, under\n   the cell id itself -- two references to one cell share the one\n   entry, and one ref per cell keeps its own (S7.5.3). A response\n   route is no exception: it lowers under its own fresh key, never\n   at a fixed key, so distinct routes stay distinct and no migrated\n   route can ever misroute a new call.\"\n  [streams cells]\n  (into {}\n        (map (fn [[cid cell]]\n               [cid {:stream-id (streams (cell-identity cells cid)),\n                     :cursor (:yin.k/position cell)}]))\n        cells))\n\n\n(defn- link-pair-resources\n  \"The engine's fixed link keys (`module/link-request-resource`,\n   `module/link-response-resource`) mapped onto the reflections a\n   lowered link wait uses: the engine's sweep retries a\n   `:link-request` by appending at the fixed request key\n   (`module/append-link-request`) and polls a `:link-response` at the\n   fixed response key (`poll-link-response`), so a lowered link wait\n   parks where the engine looks. One pair per task: the identity of\n   each side rides the pending's own markers.\"\n  [streams resources pendings]\n  (reduce (fn [acc pending]\n            (if (contains? #{:link-request :link-response}\n                           (:yin.k/reason pending))\n              (let [req-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n                                            :dao.stream/identity])\n                    resp-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/response\n                                             :dao.stream/identity])\n                    req (get resources (streams req-id))\n                    resp (get resources (streams resp-id))]\n                (if (and req resp)\n                  (assoc acc\n                         module/link-request-resource req,\n                         module/link-response-resource resp)\n                  acc))\n              acc))\n          {}\n          pendings))\n\n\n(defn- lower-one\n  \"One pending of S7.4.3, lowered: `{:entry e}` -- the wait-set\n   entry the receiver parks, the caller's register context `ctx`\n   merged verbatim under the stream and cell fields the data\n   rebuilds -- or an outcome carrying `:yin.k/status`, naming the\n   stream identity where one is known. A wait's `:cursor-ref` names\n   its cell's own fresh key, so migrated waits poll routes no new\n   call shares.\"\n  [pending ctx streams cells]\n  (case (:yin.k/reason pending)\n    :next\n    (let [cid (:yin.k/cell pending)]\n      (if-let [sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))]\n        {:entry (merge ctx\n                       {:reason :next,\n                        :stream-id sid,\n                        :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}})}\n        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n\n    :put\n    (if-let [sid (streams (get-in pending [:yin.k/stream\n                                           :dao.stream/identity]))]\n      {:entry (merge ctx\n                     {:reason :put,\n                      :stream-id sid,\n                      :datom (:yin.k/value pending)})}\n      (unsatisfied (get-in pending [:yin.k/stream :dao.stream/identity])))\n\n    :ffi\n    (let [cid (:yin.k/cell pending)]\n      (if-let [sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))]\n        {:entry (merge ctx\n                       (cond-> {:reason :next,\n                                :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n                                :stream-id sid,\n                                :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}}\n                         (contains? pending :yin.k/op)\n                         (assoc :op (:yin.k/op pending))))}\n        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n\n    :ffi-request\n    (let [req-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n                                  :dao.stream/identity])\n          route-id (cell-identity cells (:yin.k/response-cell\n                                          pending))]\n      (if (and (streams req-id) (streams route-id))\n        {:entry (merge ctx\n                       {:reason :put,\n                        :request-sent true,\n                        :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n                        :op (:yin.k/request-op pending),\n                        :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key,\n                        :datom (apply2/request (:yin.k/call-id pending)\n                                               (:yin.k/request-op\n                                                 pending)\n                                               (:yin.k/request-args\n                                                 pending))})}\n        (unsatisfied (if (streams req-id) route-id req-id))))\n\n    (:link-request :link-response)\n    (let [reason (:yin.k/reason pending)\n          cid (:yin.k/cell pending)\n          sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))\n          req (streams (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n                                        :dao.stream/identity]))]\n      (if (and sid req)\n        {:entry (merge ctx\n                       (cond-> {:reason reason,\n                                :link-id (:yin.k/link-id pending),\n                                :name (:yin.k/name pending),\n                                :request module/link-request-resource,\n                                :response module/link-response-resource,\n                                :cursor (:yin.k/position (get cells cid))}\n                         (= :link-request reason)\n                         (assoc :envelope (:yin.k/envelope pending))))}\n        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n\n    :install\n    {:entry (merge ctx\n                   {:reason :install, :name (:yin.k/name pending)})}\n\n    {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable,\n     :yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)}))\n\n\n(defn lower-frame\n  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pendings, lowered together:\n   `{:entries [e ...], :resources table}` -- one fresh stream entry\n   per distinct served identity and one fresh cursor entry per\n   distinct UCF cell, two waiters on one cell lowering back into one\n   shared entry and independent cells staying independent (S7.5.3) --\n   with the same `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   outcomes `lift-frame` gives. Where the engine resolves a wait at\n   one of its own fixed resource keys, the lower parks the wait\n   there: a link wait's pair under `module/link-request-resource` and\n   `module/link-response-resource`, and a retained request as the\n   engine's own `:request-sent` shape on the fixed local call-in key\n   -- the receiver's own pair serves the retried request and the\n   restored response wait reads the receiver's own call-out, while\n   every response route lowers under its cell's own fresh key, never\n   at a fixed key. `contexts`, parallel to `pendings`, is each\n   entry's register context, merged verbatim.\"\n  [attach! pendings cells contexts]\n  (let [ms (distinct (remove nil?\n                             (concat (map :yin.k/stream (vals cells))\n                                     (mapcat pending-markers pendings))))\n        a (attach-all attach! ms)]\n    (if (contains? a :yin.k/status)\n      a\n      (let [lowered (mapv #(lower-one %1 %2 (:streams a) cells)\n                          pendings contexts)]\n        (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %)\n                               lowered)]\n          refusal\n          {:entries (mapv :entry lowered),\n           :resources (merge (:resources a)\n                             (cell-resources (:streams a) cells)\n                             (link-pair-resources (:streams a)\n                                                  (:resources a)\n                                                  pendings))})))))\n\n\n(defn lower-pending\n  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lowered: `{:entry e, :resources\n   table}` -- the wait-set entry `ctx`'s registers ride on, and the\n   fresh resource entries it polls through -- with the same\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable` outcomes\n   `lower-frame` gives. Frame assembly that shares cursor entries\n   across entries is `lower-frame`'s.\"\n  [attach! pending cells ctx]\n  (let [r (lower-frame attach! [pending] cells [ctx])]\n    (if (contains? r :yin.k/status)\n      r\n      (-> r\n          (assoc :entry (first (:entries r)))\n          (dissoc :entries)))))\n   `:resolve` maps one whole parked entry to its live handle plus the\n   cursor to read from -- or the value to append -- out of the VM's\n   `:resources`. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an\n   earlier round baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same\n   cursor-ref may have advanced the cell since. `:advance` commits a woken\n   reader's successor -- or recovery -- cursor to its cell before later\n   entries resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances within the round.\"\n  {:resolve (fn [resources entry]\n              (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n                (let [cursor-data (get resources (:id cursor-ref))]\n                  {:stream (or (:stream entry)\n                               (get resources (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n                   :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)})\n                {:stream (or (:stream entry) (get resources (:stream-id entry)))\n                 :value (:datom entry)}))\n   :advance (fn [resources entry cursor]\n              (let [cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))]\n                (if cursor-id\n                  (assoc-in resources [cursor-id :cursor] cursor)\n                  resources)))})\n\n  [seg pc env stack k call-id]\n  {:segment seg, :pc pc, :env env, :stack stack, :k k,\n   :call-id call-id,\n   :reason :next,\n   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n\n\n(defn semantic-restore\n  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\n\n   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n\n   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n     response reader and the machine stays blocked.\n   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n  ([base entry val]\n   (if (:request-sent entry)\n     (assoc base\n            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\n                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n                                                 (:pc entry)\n                                                 (:env entry)\n                                                 (:stack entry)\n                                                 (:k entry)\n                                                 (:call-id entry)))\n            :control nil\n            :k nil\n            :value :yin/blocked\n            :blocked? true\n            :halted? false)\n     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n       (put-registers base\n                      (:segment entry)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The design requires the carried request and response pair for an outstanding call. The current retained-call lower retries through the receiver’s fixed call-in, and `semantic-restore` later creates a response wait on its fixed call-out cursor. I’m checking the call path and tests so the ruling can name the needed handoff precisely."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | sed -n '95,145p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc | sed -n '430,475p;505,545p;600,685p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | sed -n '480,610p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '585,630p;930,950p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '515,532p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | sed -n '95,145p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc | sed -n '430,475p;505,545p;600,685p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc | sed -n '480,610p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '585,630p;930,950p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '515,532p'; nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"    95\t   between evaluations.\"\n    96\t  [vm seg pc val St E K]\n    97\t  (assoc vm\n    98\t         :control (when seg {:segment seg, :pc pc})\n    99\t         :value val\n   100\t         :stack (or St [])\n   101\t         :env E\n   102\t         :k (if (seq K) K nil)\n   103\t         :halted? (and (not (:blocked? vm)) (nil? seg))))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- response-wait-entry\n   107\t  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response:\n   108\t   the machine registers {segment pc env stack k} plus the call-out reader\n   109\t   fields, in the shape `ffi/call-response-wait-entry` gives the walker.\"\n   110\t  [seg pc env stack k call-id]\n   111\t  {:segment seg, :pc pc, :env env, :stack stack, :k k,\n   112\t   :call-id call-id,\n   113\t   :reason :next,\n   114\t   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n   115\t   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn semantic-restore\n   119\t  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\n   120\t\n   121\t   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n   122\t\n   123\t   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n   124\t     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n   125\t     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n   126\t     response reader and the machine stays blocked.\n   127\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n   128\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n   129\t     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n   130\t  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n   131\t  ([base entry val]\n   132\t   (if (:request-sent entry)\n   133\t     (assoc base\n   134\t            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\n   135\t                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n   136\t                                                 (:pc entry)\n   137\t                                                 (:env entry)\n   138\t                                                 (:stack entry)\n   139\t                                                 (:k entry)\n   140\t                                                 (:call-id entry)))\n   141\t            :control nil\n   142\t            :k nil\n   143\t            :value :yin/blocked\n   144\t            :blocked? true\n   145\t            :halted? false)\n   430\t;; =============================================================================\n   431\t\n   432\t(defn lift-frame\n   433\t  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pending entries, lifted together:\n   434\t   `{:yin.k/pending [data ...], :yin.k/cells {cell-id cell},\n   435\t   :yin.k/requires requires}` on success -- the cells minted across\n   436\t   the WHOLE set, so two waiters on one engine cursor cell lift to\n   437\t   one UCF cell (S7.5.3) and `:dao.stream.remote/v1` rides in\n   438\t   `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` when any entry names a cell; the S7.9\n   439\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` outcome, naming the stream identity where one\n   440\t   is known, when any entry's stream cannot be served or its kept\n   441\t   cursor does not survive the codec round trip; the S7.9\n   442\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` outcome when any entry names no variant.\n   443\t   Failure is total (S7.5.4): a refused leaf refuses the frame.\"\n   444\t  ([serve! resources entries]\n   445\t   (lift-frame serve! resources entries cursor-codec))\n   446\t  ([serve! resources entries codec]\n   447\t   (let [found (atom {:cells {}, :of {}})\n   448\t         mint (mint-cell serve! found)\n   449\t         lifted (mapv #(lift-one serve! resources mint codec %) entries)]\n   450\t     (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %) lifted)]\n   451\t       refusal\n   452\t       {:yin.k/pending (mapv :pending lifted),\n   453\t        :yin.k/cells (:cells @found),\n   454\t        :yin.k/requires (if (some :cell? lifted) requires {})}))))\n   455\t\n   456\t\n   457\t(defn lift-pending\n   458\t  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lifted into its own self-contained\n   459\t   cell table: `{:yin.k/pending data, :yin.k/cells {cell-id cell},\n   460\t   :yin.k/requires requires}` on success, with the same `:yin.k/\n   461\t   unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable` outcomes `lift-frame`\n   462\t   gives. Frame assembly that shares cells across entries is\n   463\t   `lift-frame`'s.\"\n   464\t  ([serve! resources entry]\n   465\t   (lift-pending serve! resources entry cursor-codec))\n   466\t  ([serve! resources entry codec]\n   467\t   (let [r (lift-frame serve! resources [entry] codec)]\n   468\t     (if (contains? r :yin.k/status)\n   469\t       r\n   470\t       (assoc r :yin.k/pending (first (:yin.k/pending r)))))))\n   471\t\n   472\t\n   473\t;; =============================================================================\n   474\t;; Lower: back into resources and a wait-set entry\n   475\t;; =============================================================================\n   505\t   included -- refuses the lower as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming that\n   506\t   identity.\"\n   507\t  [attach! markers]\n   508\t  (let [by-id (reduce (fn [m mkr]\n   509\t                        (update m (:dao.stream/identity mkr) #(or % mkr)))\n   510\t                      {} markers)]\n   511\t    (reduce-kv (fn [acc identity mkr]\n   512\t                 (if-let [h (lower-marker attach! mkr)]\n   513\t                   (let [sid (stream-id-of identity)]\n   514\t                     (-> acc\n   515\t                         (assoc-in [:streams identity] sid)\n   516\t                         (assoc-in [:resources sid] h)))\n   517\t                   (reduced (unsatisfied identity))))\n   518\t               {:streams {}, :resources {}}\n   519\t               by-id)))\n   520\t\n   521\t\n   522\t(defn- cell-resources\n   523\t  \"The fresh cursor entry each UCF cell lowers to: its carried\n   524\t   position, on the reflection of the stream the cell names, under\n   525\t   the cell id itself -- two references to one cell share the one\n   526\t   entry, and one ref per cell keeps its own (S7.5.3). A response\n   527\t   route is no exception: it lowers under its own fresh key, never\n   528\t   at a fixed key, so distinct routes stay distinct and no migrated\n   529\t   route can ever misroute a new call.\"\n   530\t  [streams cells]\n   531\t  (into {}\n   532\t        (map (fn [[cid cell]]\n   533\t               [cid {:stream-id (streams (cell-identity cells cid)),\n   534\t                     :cursor (:yin.k/position cell)}]))\n   535\t        cells))\n   536\t\n   537\t\n   538\t(defn- link-pair-resources\n   539\t  \"The engine's fixed link keys (`module/link-request-resource`,\n   540\t   `module/link-response-resource`) mapped onto the reflections a\n   541\t   lowered link wait uses: the engine's sweep retries a\n   542\t   `:link-request` by appending at the fixed request key\n   543\t   (`module/append-link-request`) and polls a `:link-response` at the\n   544\t   fixed response key (`poll-link-response`), so a lowered link wait\n   545\t   parks where the engine looks. One pair per task: the identity of\n   600\t                                :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n   601\t                                :stream-id sid,\n   602\t                                :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}}\n   603\t                         (contains? pending :yin.k/op)\n   604\t                         (assoc :op (:yin.k/op pending))))}\n   605\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   606\t\n   607\t    :ffi-request\n   608\t    (let [req-id (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n   609\t                                  :dao.stream/identity])\n   610\t          route-id (cell-identity cells (:yin.k/response-cell\n   611\t                                          pending))]\n   612\t      (if (and (streams req-id) (streams route-id))\n   613\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   614\t                       {:reason :put,\n   615\t                        :request-sent true,\n   616\t                        :call-id (:yin.k/call-id pending),\n   617\t                        :op (:yin.k/request-op pending),\n   618\t                        :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key,\n   619\t                        :datom (apply2/request (:yin.k/call-id pending)\n   620\t                                               (:yin.k/request-op\n   621\t                                                 pending)\n   622\t                                               (:yin.k/request-args\n   623\t                                                 pending))})}\n   624\t        (unsatisfied (if (streams req-id) route-id req-id))))\n   625\t\n   626\t    (:link-request :link-response)\n   627\t    (let [reason (:yin.k/reason pending)\n   628\t          cid (:yin.k/cell pending)\n   629\t          sid (streams (cell-identity cells cid))\n   630\t          req (streams (get-in pending [:yin.k/request\n   631\t                                        :dao.stream/identity]))]\n   632\t      (if (and sid req)\n   633\t        {:entry (merge ctx\n   634\t                       (cond-> {:reason reason,\n   635\t                                :link-id (:yin.k/link-id pending),\n   636\t                                :name (:yin.k/name pending),\n   637\t                                :request module/link-request-resource,\n   638\t                                :response module/link-response-resource,\n   639\t                                :cursor (:yin.k/position (get cells cid))}\n   640\t                         (= :link-request reason)\n   641\t                         (assoc :envelope (:yin.k/envelope pending))))}\n   642\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   643\t\n   644\t    :install\n   645\t    {:entry (merge ctx\n   646\t                   {:reason :install, :name (:yin.k/name pending)})}\n   647\t\n   648\t    {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable,\n   649\t     :yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)}))\n   650\t\n   651\t\n   652\t(defn lower-frame\n   653\t  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pendings, lowered together:\n   654\t   `{:entries [e ...], :resources table}` -- one fresh stream entry\n   655\t   per distinct served identity and one fresh cursor entry per\n   656\t   distinct UCF cell, two waiters on one cell lowering back into one\n   657\t   shared entry and independent cells staying independent (S7.5.3) --\n   658\t   with the same `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   659\t   outcomes `lift-frame` gives. Where the engine resolves a wait at\n   660\t   one of its own fixed resource keys, the lower parks the wait\n   661\t   there: a link wait's pair under `module/link-request-resource` and\n   662\t   `module/link-response-resource`, and a retained request as the\n   663\t   engine's own `:request-sent` shape on the fixed local call-in key\n   664\t   -- the receiver's own pair serves the retried request and the\n   665\t   restored response wait reads the receiver's own call-out, while\n   666\t   every response route lowers under its cell's own fresh key, never\n   667\t   at a fixed key. `contexts`, parallel to `pendings`, is each\n   668\t   entry's register context, merged verbatim.\"\n   669\t  [attach! pendings cells contexts]\n   670\t  (let [ms (distinct (remove nil?\n   671\t                             (concat (map :yin.k/stream (vals cells))\n   672\t                                     (mapcat pending-markers pendings))))\n   673\t        a (attach-all attach! ms)]\n   674\t    (if (contains? a :yin.k/status)\n   675\t      a\n   676\t      (let [lowered (mapv #(lower-one %1 %2 (:streams a) cells)\n   677\t                          pendings contexts)]\n   678\t        (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %)\n   679\t                               lowered)]\n   680\t          refusal\n   681\t          {:entries (mapv :entry lowered),\n   682\t           :resources (merge (:resources a)\n   683\t                             (cell-resources (:streams a) cells)\n   684\t                             (link-pair-resources (:streams a)\n   685\t                                                  (:resources a)\n   480\t      ;; the linker answered before the migration: the response sits\n   481\t      ;; on the emitter's response stream, past the kept cursor\n   482\t      (stream/append! resp-src\n   483\t                      {:status :ok,\n   484\t                       :yin.link/id (:link-id entry),\n   485\t                       :manifest {:yin.module/name 'host.the-module}})\n   486\t      (is (vm/blocked? (vm/run receiver))\n   487\t          \"first poll: the envelope is retried, the answer not yet read\")\n   488\t      (let [w0 (:dao.stream/cursor\n   489\t                 (stream/cursor req-src :dao.stream/oldest))]\n   490\t        (is (= :warmed\n   491\t               (:dao.stream/value (stream/next req-src w0)))\n   492\t            \"reading the warm-up frees the slot the retry needs\"))\n   493\t      (serve-mirror! peer)\n   494\t      (let [c0 (:dao.stream/cursor\n   495\t                 (stream/cursor req-src :dao.stream/oldest))\n   496\t            r (stream/next req-src c0)]\n   497\t        (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r)))\n   498\t        (is (= (:envelope entry) (:dao.stream/value r))\n   499\t            \"the envelope verbatim, appended via the fixed request key\"))\n   500\t      (let [done (vm/run receiver)]\n   501\t        (is (vm/halted? done))\n   502\t        (is (= 'host.the-module (vm/value done))\n   503\t            \"the require resumes with the module's own value\")\n   504\t        (is (empty? (:wait-set done)))))))\n   505\t\n   506\t\n   507\t(deftest a-retained-ffi-request-migrates-and-the-retry-resumes\n   508\t  (let [t (toy)\n   509\t        peer (served-peer t)\n   510\t        serve! (server peer (:channel t) #{:reader :writer})\n   511\t        attach! (attacher t)\n   512\t        program (then (call-node :op/echo \"hello\")\n   513\t                      (call-node :op/echo \"world\"))\n   514\t        [datoms parked entry call-in-src call-out-src]\n   515\t        (parked-caller program)\n   516\t        kept (get-in parked [:resources vm/call-out-cursor-key :cursor])\n   517\t        lifted (ucf.remote/lift-pending serve! (:resources parked) entry)\n   518\t        pending (:yin.k/pending lifted)\n   519\t        lowered (ucf.remote/lower-pending attach! pending\n   520\t                                          (:yin.k/cells lifted)\n   521\t                                          (registers-of entry))\n   522\t        e (:entry lowered)\n   523\t        rs (:resources lowered)\n   524\t        ;; the resumer's own pair: the receiving composition supplies\n   525\t        ;; it under the fixed stream keys, and it serves the retried\n   526\t        ;; request and every call the resumed program makes\n   527\t        own-call-in (ring 8)\n   528\t        own-call-out (ring 8)\n   529\t        own (calling-machine own-call-in own-call-out)\n   530\t        own-pair (select-keys (:resources own)\n   531\t                              [vm/call-in-stream-key\n   532\t                               vm/call-out-stream-key\n   533\t                               vm/call-out-cursor-key])\n   534\t        receiver (-> (quiesced-receiver datoms lowered own)\n   535\t                     (update :resources #(merge own-pair %)))\n   536\t        oldest (fn [h]\n   537\t                 (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor h :dao.stream/oldest)))\n   538\t        ;; phase 1: the sweep retries the retained request on the\n   539\t        ;; receiver's own call-in and the restored response wait parks\n   540\t        r1 (vm/run receiver)\n   541\t        a-request (stream/next own-call-in (oldest own-call-in))\n   542\t        a-response (apply2/success-response\n   543\t                     (apply2/request-id (:dao.stream/value a-request))\n   544\t                     \"echo!\")\n   545\t        _ (stream/append! own-call-out a-response)\n   546\t        ;; phase 2: the migrated call resumes and the program's own\n   547\t        ;; next call parks on the receiver's own pair -- no manual\n   548\t        ;; handoff anywhere\n   549\t        r2 (vm/run r1)\n   550\t        b-request (stream/next own-call-in\n   551\t                               (:dao.stream/cursor a-request))\n   552\t        b-response (apply2/success-response\n   553\t                     (apply2/request-id (:dao.stream/value b-request))\n   554\t                     \"world!\")\n   555\t        _ (stream/append! own-call-out b-response)\n   556\t        ;; phase 3: the second call resumes; the program halts\n   557\t        r3 (vm/run r2)]\n   558\t    (testing \"the machine parked through the engine, request retained\"\n   559\t      (is (vm/blocked? parked))\n   560\t      (is (true? (:request-sent entry)))\n   561\t      (is (= vm/call-in-stream-key (:stream-id entry)))\n   562\t      (is (apply2/request? (:datom entry))))\n   563\t    (testing \"the lift carries the response route as a cell\"\n   564\t      (is (= :yin.k/c-0 (:yin.k/response-cell pending)))\n   565\t      (is (= kept\n   566\t             (get-in lifted [:yin.k/cells :yin.k/c-0 :yin.k/position]))\n   567\t          \"the emitter's call-out cursor at its kept position\"))\n   568\t    (testing \"the lower keeps the engine's own shape, pair untouched\"\n   569\t      (is (= vm/call-in-stream-key (:stream-id e))\n   570\t          \"the engine's own retained shape: the receiver's own pair\n   571\t           serves the retried request\")\n   572\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-in-stream-key))\n   573\t          \"the pair itself is the composition's own supply, never\n   574\t           lowered\")\n   575\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-out-stream-key)))\n   576\t      (is (not (contains? rs vm/call-out-cursor-key))\n   577\t          \"no route is ever installed at the fixed call-out cursor\")\n   578\t      (let [route (get rs :yin.k/c-0)]\n   579\t        (is (= kept (:cursor route))\n   580\t            \"the route cell lowers as its own fresh cursor entry\")\n   581\t        (is (not= vm/call-out-stream-key (:stream-id route))\n   582\t            \"naming the emitter's call-out reflection, never the\n   583\t             receiver's key\")\n   584\t        (is (contains? rs (:stream-id route))\n   585\t            \"which is attached, so a co-waiting response wait resolves\n   586\t             it\")))\n   587\t    (testing \"the retried request is served by the receiver's own pair\"\n   588\t      (is (vm/blocked? r1)\n   589\t          \"first round: the request is retried, the response not yet\n   590\t           read\")\n   591\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome a-request)))\n   592\t      (is (= (:datom entry) (:dao.stream/value a-request))\n   593\t          \"the retained request verbatim, on the receiver's own\n   594\t           call-in\")\n   595\t      (is (vm/blocked? r2)\n   596\t          \"the migrated call resumes and the program calls again\")\n   597\t      (is (= :next (:reason (first (:wait-set r2))))\n   598\t          \"the program's own new call is the one waiting now\")\n   599\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome b-request)))\n   600\t      (is (= [\"world\"] (apply2/request-args (:dao.stream/value b-request)))\n   601\t          \"the new call's request is on the receiver's own call-in too\"))\n   602\t    (testing \"nothing crossed to the emitter's pair\"\n   603\t      (is (= :warmed\n   604\t             (:dao.stream/value\n   605\t               (stream/next call-in-src (oldest call-in-src))))\n   606\t          \"the emitter's call-in holds its warm-up and no request\")\n   607\t      (is (= :dao.stream/blocked\n   608\t             (:dao.stream/outcome\n   609\t               (stream/next call-out-src (oldest call-out-src))))\n   610\t          \"and the emitter's call-out holds no response\"))\n   585\t\n   586\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   587\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   588\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   589\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   590\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   591\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   592\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   593\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   594\t  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\n   595\t  cell through `dao.stream.remote.md`: the exporter enters the cell's\n   596\t  handle in its table and the cell's stream descriptor becomes a remote\n   597\t  descriptor. This is an access path to the original logical stream, not\n   598\t  a copied stream with new positions. `dao.stream` itself assumes no\n   599\t  network. The mirror serves the source's declared reader and writer\n   600\t  surface, preserving its outcome maps and cursor namespace rather than\n   601\t  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n   602\t  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\n   603\t  cursor. `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` names\n   604\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, the one profile: a cursor is plain data in the\n   605\t  channel's portable domain and the source honors it after\n   606\t  serialization, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n   607\t  If the source cannot be entered in a table, preserve the cursor, or\n   608\t  keep a channel reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n   609\t  minting the value. A reflection marked gone on the receiver is also\n   610\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity. The exporter keeps\n   611\t  the entry served under a lease the resumer holds\n   612\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6). Re-homing a stream is out of\n   613\t  scope: serving from the exporter pins the exporter.\n   614\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   615\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   616\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   617\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   618\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   619\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   620\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   621\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   622\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   623\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   624\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   625\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   626\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   627\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   628\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   629\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   630\t  value), where through a reflection `ok` is acceptance on the outbound\n   930\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   931\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   932\t```\n   933\t\n   934\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   935\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   936\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   937\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   938\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   939\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   940\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   941\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   942\t\n   943\t**Cells lower into the private resource table (linker M4).** Specified by\n   944\t`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The lowering rule above -- each\n   945\tcell becomes a fresh store cursor entry -- is amended by the resource\n   946\tsplit of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3: each cell becomes a cursor entry in the\n   947\treceiver's private `:resources` table, seeded with its carried position,\n   948\tand every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one\n   949\tcell still share one entry and one ref per cell keeps its own, but no\n   950\tstore key is ever created, and program values keep only the opaque\n   515\t   `:resolve` maps one whole parked entry to its live handle plus the\n   516\t   cursor to read from -- or the value to append -- out of the VM's\n   517\t   `:resources`. The cursor is re-resolved on every round even when an\n   518\t   earlier round baked a value into the entry: another waiter on the same\n   519\t   cursor-ref may have advanced the cell since. `:advance` commits a woken\n   520\t   reader's successor -- or recovery -- cursor to its cell before later\n   521\t   entries resolve, so a shared cursor-ref advances within the round.\"\n   522\t  {:resolve (fn [resources entry]\n   523\t              (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n   524\t                (let [cursor-data (get resources (:id cursor-ref))]\n   525\t                  {:stream (or (:stream entry)\n   526\t                               (get resources (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n   527\t                   :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)})\n   528\t                {:stream (or (:stream entry) (get resources (:stream-id entry)))\n   529\t                 :value (:datom entry)}))\n   530\t   :advance (fn [resources entry cursor]\n   531\t              (let [cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))]\n   532\t                (if cursor-id\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n    19\t   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n    20\t   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n    21\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    22\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    25\t\n    26\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    27\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    28\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    29\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    30\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    31\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    34\t\n    35\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    36\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    37\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    38\t\n    39\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    40\t\n    41\t## Streams\n    42\t\n    43\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    44\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    45\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    46\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    47\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    48\t\n    49\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    50\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    51\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    52\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    53\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    54\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    55\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    56\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    57\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    58\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    59\tthe stream's own.\n    60\t\n    61\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    62\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    63\t\n    64\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    65\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 22:17:10 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 05:17:10 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Ruling: lower a retained FFI call only when its carried pair can be re-established.** The retry goes to the reflection attached from `:yin.k/request`; its response wait reads the reflection attached from `:yin.k/response` through the carried response cell at its kept cursor. If either endpoint cannot be served at lift or attached at lower, return `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming that endpoint’s identity. Preserve both route markers and the cell in the UCF entry so a later reachable host can try lowering the same value. Do not retry through the receiver’s handler. UCF requires the carried pair for an outstanding call and reserves the receiver’s fixed pair for future calls ([UCF:565](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:565), [UCF:594](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:594), [UCF:614](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:614)).\n\nUse an allocated private resource key for the request reflection as the retained `:put` entry’s `:stream-id`. Use another allocated key for the response reflection as the response cursor resource’s `:stream-id`; the resulting `:next` entry’s `:cursor-ref :id` is the allocated key for that cell. Its `:stream-id` should name the response reflection as well. The engine resolves writes by the entry’s `:stream-id`, and reads through the cursor resource’s `:stream-id` ([engine.cljc:522](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:522)). The present lower instead selects `vm/call-in-stream-key` ([remote.cljc:607](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:607)).\n\n**Required separate dispatch:** `semantic-restore` currently turns every successful retained request into a wait on `vm/call-out-cursor-key`, regardless of route data on the writer ([semantic.cljc:106](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:106), [semantic.cljc:132](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:132)). It must carry the retained writer’s response reflection and cursor keys into the new response wait, while ordinary new calls continue to use the fixed keys. The two slice-8 files can encode and lower those keys, but **cannot deliver correct end-to-end retained-call resume within the stated file boundary**. Do not claim that acceptance gate closed until that semantic change lands.\n\n**Fresh-key rule:** Treat each lowering as a frame batch. Allocate one private resource key per distinct UCF cell ID in that batch and remap every reference to it to that key. Allocate stream reflection keys in the same collision-free batch allocation. A repeated cell ID within the batch shares one cursor resource; equal IDs in separately lowered frames get different keys. Check proposed keys against the receiver’s existing `:resources`, all fixed VM resource keys, and keys already allocated in the batch; regenerate on collision. A prefix or stream identity derived key alone does not establish freshness. The lower API or its composition must receive the occupied key set or a receiver scoped allocator before installation. Current lift restarts IDs at `:yin.k/c-0`, while lower uses the cell ID directly and derives stream keys from identity ([remote.cljc:447](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:447), [remote.cljc:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:477), [remote.cljc:522](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:522)); UCF explicitly requires fresh remapping with same-cell sharing ([UCF:934](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:934)).\n\n**Tests to pin:** retained-call lift carries both endpoint markers, the exact retained envelope data, and the kept response cell; unavailable request or response yields `:yin.k/unsatisfied` without receiver-handler fallback; lower writes through the carried request reflection and gives the response wait the carried cell route; a different receiver handler cannot determine the migrated result; subsequent calls use its local pair; co-waiters on one cell advance in order while independent equal-position cells remain independent; two separately lowered frames with `:yin.k/c-0` and collisions against pre-existing resources retain distinct, working cursor entries. Replace the current test’s expectation that the retry reaches the receiver’s call-in ([remote_test.cljc:507](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:507), [remote_test.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:568)). End-to-end successful retry followed by the carried response wait is a gate for the separate `semantic.cljc` dispatch, consistent with UCF §7.11’s pending-state blocker ([UCF:1596](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1596)).\n\nNo files were edited.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE"}}
